CIS container selftests (development status)
============================================

Run only in the disposable ARM64 VM created for this task. The host's running
kernel and other containers are not test fixtures. Never use global pkill,
docker prune, host reboot or unscoped cgroup deletion.

Build: make LDFLAGS=-static
Local logic: ./logic_test
Offline logic: python3 test_attribution.py; python3 test_acceptance.py
Guest lifecycle: /identity metrics; /identity ip
Guest fleet: /fleet COUNT off|metrics|ip|diag bench|open-loop SECONDS TARGET
Guest controls: /fleet 2 ip cpu-compete 14 0; /fleet 2 ip quota 14 0
Guest reclaim: /fleet 1 diag reclaim 3 0
Guest idle registration: /fleet 128 ip idle 3 0; /fleet 256 ip idle 3 0

The guest must mount cgroup2, proc, sysfs and tracefs, enable the CPU and memory
controllers for test children, and make the tested BPF program and binaries
available at /cisd, /cis.bpf.o and /container-root/workload. The immutable
container root includes sample, proc and tmp. All business programs execute as
PID 1 inside new PID/mount/network/IPC/UTS/cgroup namespaces. The host helper is
not counted as a business container. This minimal namespace runtime is not OCI.

Build fixture/cis_fixture.ko against the exact tested Image and load it inside
the VM with isolated_vm=1. Copy only its misc device node into the test root.
Run fixture-shared, fixture-private and async-fixture fleets with two containers
and ip mode. They request short diagnostics explicitly. Kernel mutex fixtures
are not user-futex substitutes. The current protocol uses 200 operations per
container and 800 total shared/private truth records; old 100-operation pilot
logs retain their original checker/version and are not silently reinterpreted.

Collect every stdout record and cisd JSONL only after measurement. Each child
writes its own log; the parent merges completed logs to avoid interleaving.
Check actual IP/interval samples, not only process exit codes:

  python3 sample_check.py identity.log identity-samples.json
  python3 truth_check.py mutex.log mutex-truth.json
  python3 async_check.py async.log async-truth.json

Fixed-arrival latency includes dispatch delay and backlog; a memory.high test
can take longer than its requested duration because the final allocation must
finish. Such time is retained, not truncated into an artificial fast result.

The S6 plan generator refuses admission if S2 overhead/coverage has not passed.
Diagnostic timing must be checked against attachment windows; a two-second
collector cannot be averaged over a 15-second workload to claim <=3% impact.
No script exit status alone marks S0-S6 complete. See acceptance.py and the
artifact-hashed result inventory. Unsupported hardware does not waive core
coverage, correctness or performance requirements.
